Equal objects must return the same hashCode, equals must be reflexive, symmetric, transitive and consistent, and x.equals(null) must be false; breaking the hashCode half makes HashMap and HashSet lose objects.

public final class OrderLine {
    private final String sku;
    private final int quantity;
    public OrderLine(String sku, int quantity) {
        this.sku = sku;
        this.quantity = quantity;
    }
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof OrderLine other)) return false;
        return quantity == other.quantity && Objects.equals(sku, other.sku);
    }
    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(sku, quantity);
    }
}

Both methods use exactly the same fields, which keeps them consistent. The instanceof pattern is safe because the class is final; in a non-final hierarchy, comparing getClass() avoids symmetry bugs between parent and subclass. Both checks are O(1) time and space for these fields. The classic bug is overriding equals only: two equal lines then hash to different buckets, so set.contains returns false and duplicates pile up. Fields should be immutable, otherwise a key mutated after insertion becomes unreachable. In Java 16+ a record OrderLine(String sku, int quantity) generates both methods correctly, which is what I would use for a value type. For JPA entities, base equality on a business key or a non-null id, not on lazy associations.

A record gives final fields, a canonical constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode and toString, but it is only shallowly immutable, so validate and defensively copy in a compact constructor.

public record Invoice(String id, List<BigDecimal> amounts, Currency currency) {
    public Invoice {
        Objects.requireNonNull(id, "id");
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, "currency");
        amounts = List.copyOf(amounts);
        if (amounts.stream().anyMatch(a -> a.signum() < 0)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative amount");
        }
    }
    public BigDecimal total() {
        return amounts.stream().reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
    }
}

List.copyOf returns an unmodifiable list and rejects null elements, so a caller who later mutates their original ArrayList cannot change the invoice, and calling add on amounts() throws UnsupportedOperationException. Construction is O(n) time and O(n) space for the copy; total is O(n) time. BigDecimal is itself immutable, which is why a shallow copy is enough; a list of mutable objects would need deep copies. Immutability makes the object safe to share across threads and to cache, and Jackson deserialises records through the canonical constructor since version 2.12. Pitfall: records cannot extend classes and are a poor fit for JPA entities, which need a no-arg constructor and mutable state, so use them for DTOs, events and value objects.

Apply producer-extends, consumer-super: the source produces T values so it is List<? extends T>, and the destination consumes them so it is List<? super T>.

public static <T> void copyAll(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dst) {
    for (T item : src) {
        dst.add(item);
    }
}

List<Integer> ids = List.of(1, 2, 3);
List<Number> sink = new ArrayList<>();
copyAll(ids, sink);

With plain List<T> on both sides the call fails, because generics are invariant: List<Integer> is not a List<Number>, otherwise you could add a Double to a list of integers. From a List<? extends Number> you can read Number but can only add null; into a List<? super Integer> you can add Integer but only read Object. Time is O(n) and extra space O(1) beyond growth of the destination. Generics are erased at runtime, so you cannot write new T[]; pass a Class<T> or an IntFunction<T[]> when you need the runtime type. The JDK follows the same rule in Collections.copy and in sort methods that take Comparator<? super T>. Guideline for public APIs: use wildcards in parameters to make callers' lives easy, but never in return types, because that pushes wildcard handling onto every caller.

A sealed interface with record implementations lets each outcome carry only its own data, and a switch over it is checked for exhaustiveness by the compiler, so adding a new outcome breaks the build instead of production.

public sealed interface PaymentResult
        permits Approved, Declined, PendingReview {}
public record Approved(String authCode) implements PaymentResult {}
public record Declined(String reason, boolean retryable) implements PaymentResult {}
public record PendingReview(Duration eta) implements PaymentResult {}

static int httpStatus(PaymentResult r) {
    return switch (r) {
        case Approved a -> 201;
        case Declined(String reason, boolean retryable) when retryable -> 503;
        case Declined d -> 402;
        case PendingReview p -> 202;
    };
}

There is no default branch, so if someone adds a Refunded record to the permits list, every switch like this fails to compile until it is handled. Record patterns deconstruct components inline and the when guard refines a case; order matters, because the compiler rejects a case that is dominated by an earlier unguarded one. Dispatch is O(1) per call with no extra space. Compared with an enum plus nullable authCode and reason fields, illegal states such as an approved result carrying a decline reason cannot be represented. A null argument throws NullPointerException unless you add case null. For REST payloads, Jackson needs @JsonTypeInfo and @JsonSubTypes to serialise the hierarchy polymorphically.

Split on a whitespace regex after strip, then append the words in reverse to a StringBuilder; strings are immutable, so += inside a loop copies the growing string every iteration and turns linear work quadratic.

public static String reverseWords(String input) {
    if (input == null || input.isBlank()) return "";
    String[] words = input.strip().split("\\s+");
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(input.length());
    for (int i = words.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        sb.append(words[i]);
        if (i > 0) sb.append(' ');
    }
    return sb.toString();
}

Time is O(n) in the input length and space O(n) for the array and builder. strip is Unicode-aware, unlike trim, which only removes characters up to U+0020, and isBlank exists since Java 11. Since Java 9, javac compiles a single a + b + c expression into an efficient invokedynamic call, so concatenation within one expression is fine; the problem is repeated concatenation across loop iterations. Related String rules: compare with equals, never ==, because == checks reference identity and only works by accident for interned literals; use toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT) to avoid locale bugs such as the Turkish dotless i; and pass StandardCharsets.UTF_8 explicitly when converting bytes to text. String.split with a regex compiles the pattern on every call, so precompile a Pattern in hot paths.

Start both calls with supplyAsync on a dedicated executor, apply a timeout to each, and combine with thenCombine so total latency is roughly the slower call rather than the sum.

private final ExecutorService io = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();

public OrderView details(String orderId) {
    CompletableFuture<Price> price = CompletableFuture
        .supplyAsync(() -> pricing.get(orderId), io)
        .orTimeout(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    CompletableFuture<Stock> stock = CompletableFuture
        .supplyAsync(() -> inventory.get(orderId), io)
        .completeOnTimeout(Stock.UNKNOWN, 2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
        .exceptionally(ex -> Stock.UNKNOWN);
    return price.thenCombine(stock, OrderView::new).join();
}

Pricing is mandatory, so its timeout fails the request with a TimeoutException wrapped in CompletionException, which a @RestControllerAdvice can map to 504. Inventory degrades to UNKNOWN on timeout or error. Without the executor argument, supplyAsync uses ForkJoinPool.commonPool, which is sized to cores minus one and shared JVM-wide, so blocking HTTP calls there starve parallel streams and other async work. On Java 21 a virtual-thread executor suits blocking I/O; on older JDKs use a bounded ThreadPoolExecutor. Pitfalls: orTimeout does not cancel the underlying HTTP call, so also set connect and read timeouts on RestClient or WebClient, and MDC or trace context is not propagated automatically, so use Micrometer context propagation. Wall time is about the maximum of the two calls, with O(1) extra space.

Use ConcurrentHashMap.merge or computeIfAbsent, which perform the read-modify-write atomically per key; a separate get followed by put is two operations, so two threads can read the same old value and one increment is lost.

public final class CustomerCounter {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, LongAdder> counts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public void record(String customerId) {
        counts.computeIfAbsent(customerId, k -> new LongAdder()).increment();
    }

    public long count(String customerId) {
        LongAdder adder = counts.get(customerId);
        return adder == null ? 0 : adder.sum();
    }
}

The simpler counts.merge(customerId, 1L, Long::sum) on a ConcurrentHashMap<String, Long> is also correct; the LongAdder version reduces contention when a few hot keys receive most updates. Each record call is O(1) average time, and space is O(k) for k distinct customers. Internally, since Java 8, ConcurrentHashMap uses CAS to insert into empty bins and synchronises only on the head node of a bin for updates, so threads touching different bins rarely block. Keep compute and computeIfAbsent functions short and never modify the same map inside them, because they run while holding the bin lock and recursive updates can throw IllegalStateException or hang. Null keys and values are rejected, and size() and iteration are weakly consistent, which is fine for metrics but not for exact invariants.

Validate with Jakarta Bean Validation on a record, return ResponseEntity.created, and extend ResponseEntityExceptionHandler so framework errors and your own exceptions all render as application/problem+json.

record CreateOrderRequest(@NotBlank String customerId, @Positive int quantity) {}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/orders")
class OrderController {
    private final OrderService service;
    OrderController(OrderService service) { this.service = service; }

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<OrderDto> create(@Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        OrderDto dto = service.create(req);
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/v1/orders/" + dto.id())).body(dto);
    }
}

@RestControllerAdvice
class ApiErrors extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(OrderNotFoundException.class)
    ProblemDetail notFound(OrderNotFoundException ex) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
        pd.setTitle("Order not found");
        pd.setProperty("errorCode", "ORDER_NOT_FOUND");
        return pd;
    }
}

A failed @Valid throws MethodArgumentNotValidException, which the base class already turns into a 400 ProblemDetail; override handleMethodArgumentNotValid to add a fieldErrors property so the React or Angular form can highlight fields. Setting spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true gives the same format without a custom advice. Use 201 for create, 404 for missing resources, 409 for state conflicts and 422 only if your team agrees on it for business-rule failures. Pitfall: forgetting @Valid on the parameter means constraints never run. Verify with a @WebMvcTest asserting status and jsonPath("$.title").

Persist the key with a unique constraint before doing the work, then replay the stored response for retries and reject reuse of the same key with a different body.

public PaymentDto pay(String key, PaymentRequest req) {
    String hash = fingerprint(req); // SHA-256 of canonical JSON
    if (store.tryInsert(key, hash)) {
        try {
            PaymentDto result = gateway.charge(req, key);
            store.complete(key, toJson(result));
            return result;
        } catch (RuntimeException ex) {
            store.delete(key);
            throw ex;
        }
    }
    IdemRecord existing = store.find(key).orElseThrow();
    if (!existing.requestHash().equals(hash)) {
        throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, "Key reused with a different body");
    }
    if ("IN_PROGRESS".equals(existing.status())) {
        throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, "Original request still processing");
    }
    return fromJson(existing.responseJson(), PaymentDto.class);
}

tryInsert runs INSERT INTO idempotency_keys (idem_key, request_hash, status) VALUES (?, ?, 'IN_PROGRESS') ON CONFLICT (idem_key) DO NOTHING in its own short transaction and returns true when one row was inserted, so the database, not application code, settles races between concurrent retries. The controller reads @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key"). Passing the same key to the payment provider gives end-to-end protection, since providers such as Stripe accept an Idempotency-Key header. Do not wrap the external call in one long database transaction, which holds connections and row locks. Expire keys after 24 to 72 hours with a scheduled cleanup. Verify by firing two identical requests concurrently in a Testcontainers test and asserting exactly one charge.

Add spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server, point it at the issuer, and supply a SecurityFilterChain with a JwtAuthenticationConverter that reads the roles claim.

@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity
class SecurityConfig {
    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain api(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
            .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/actuator/health/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/v1/orders/**").hasRole("order-writer")
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o.jwt(jwt -> jwt.jwtAuthenticationConverter(rolesConverter())))
            .build();
    }

    private JwtAuthenticationConverter rolesConverter() {
        var authorities = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();
        authorities.setAuthoritiesClaimName("roles");
        authorities.setAuthorityPrefix("ROLE_");
        var converter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();
        converter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(authorities);
        return converter;
    }
}

In application.yml set spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri so Boot discovers the JWKS endpoint and validates signature, exp and iss, and set spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences so tokens minted for other apps are rejected. Disabling CSRF is safe only because the API is stateless and uses bearer tokens, not cookies. Without the converter, Spring maps the scope claim to SCOPE_ authorities, which is the usual reason hasRole checks return 403. Use @PreAuthorize for method-level rules. Test with MockMvc and SecurityMockMvcRequestPostProcessors.jwt().authorities(...) rather than real tokens, and expect 401 for a missing token and 403 for a wrong role.

Fetch the association in the same query, either with JPQL JOIN FETCH or with @EntityGraph on a derived query method.

public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    @Query("select o from Order o join fetch o.lines where o.customerId = :customerId")
    List<Order> findWithLines(@Param("customerId") String customerId);

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"lines", "customer"})
    List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);

    @Query("select o.id from Order o where o.status = :status")
    Page<Long> findIdsByStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = "lines")
    List<Order> findByIdIn(Collection<Long> ids);
}

Hibernate 6 deduplicates parent rows from a collection join automatically, so distinct is no longer required. Pagination is the trap: combining a collection fetch with Pageable makes Hibernate log HHH90003004 and apply LIMIT in memory after loading every row, so fetch a page of ids first with findIdsByStatus, then load those ids with findByIdIn and reorder to match. A simpler alternative is spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size=50, which turns N lazy loads into a few IN queries. Fetching two List collections at once throws MultipleBagFetchException; use Set or separate queries. Set hibernate.query.fail_on_pagination_over_collection_fetch=true to turn the in-memory warning into an error. Verify by enabling hibernate.generate_statistics or asserting the statement count with a datasource-proxy listener in a test, so the regression cannot return silently.

Mock the collaborators with MockitoExtension, stub the decline, assert the exception, and verify the repository was never called.

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {
    @Mock OrderRepository repository;
    @Mock PaymentClient paymentClient;
    @InjectMocks OrderService service;

    @Test
    void doesNotSaveWhenPaymentDeclined() {
        when(paymentClient.charge(any())).thenReturn(PaymentResult.declined("insufficient funds"));

        assertThrows(PaymentDeclinedException.class,
            () -> service.place(new CreateOrderRequest("c-42", 2)));

        verify(repository, never()).save(any());
    }

    @Test
    void savesWhenPaymentApproved() {
        when(paymentClient.charge(any())).thenReturn(PaymentResult.approved("tx-1"));
        when(repository.save(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> inv.getArgument(0));

        Order order = service.place(new CreateOrderRequest("c-42", 2));

        assertEquals(OrderStatus.PAID, order.getStatus());
    }
}

No Spring context is started, so these run in milliseconds. MockitoExtension uses strict stubs, which fails the test with UnnecessaryStubbingException when a stub is never used, a useful signal that the test no longer matches the code. Prefer constructor injection in OrderService so @InjectMocks, or a plain new OrderService(repository, paymentClient), works predictably. Use ArgumentCaptor when you need to assert what was saved, and @ParameterizedTest with @CsvSource to cover several decline reasons without copy-paste. Avoid mocking value objects or the class under test.

Start real containers, wire them in with @ServiceConnection, publish an event, and wait asynchronously for the database state with Awaitility.

@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class ShipmentFlowIT {
    @Container @ServiceConnection
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @Container @ServiceConnection
    static KafkaContainer kafka = new KafkaContainer("apache/kafka:3.8.0");

    @Autowired OrderRepository repository;
    @Autowired KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    @Test
    void shipmentEventMarksOrderShipped() {
        Order saved = repository.save(Order.pending("c-42"));

        kafkaTemplate.send("shipments", saved.getId().toString(),
            "{\"orderId\":" + saved.getId() + "}");

        await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(15)).untilAsserted(() ->
            assertThat(repository.findById(saved.getId()))
                .get().extracting(Order::getStatus).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.SHIPPED));
    }
}

KafkaContainer here is org.testcontainers.kafka.KafkaContainer from Testcontainers 1.20, and @ServiceConnection, available since Spring Boot 3.1, sets the datasource URL and spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers without @DynamicPropertySource. Flyway runs against the real PostgreSQL, so migrations are tested too. Static containers are shared across test methods; to share across classes, put them in a base class or a @TestConfiguration, and enable reuse locally with testcontainers.reuse.enable=true. Never use Thread.sleep: Awaitility polls until the consumer commits, which keeps the test fast and stable. Set spring.kafka.consumer.auto-offset-reset=earliest so the listener sees messages sent before it was assigned partitions. CI runners need Docker access, or Testcontainers Cloud.

Register a DefaultErrorHandler with a DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer and a backoff, and mark non-retryable exceptions so they go straight to the DLT.

@Bean
DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(KafkaTemplate<Object, Object> template) {
    var recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template,
        (rec, ex) -> new TopicPartition(rec.topic() + ".DLT", rec.partition()));
    var backoff = new ExponentialBackOff(1000L, 2.0);
    backoff.setMaxElapsedTime(10000L);
    var handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backoff);
    handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(ValidationException.class);
    return handler;
}

Spring Boot wires a single CommonErrorHandler bean into the auto-configured listener container factory. The handler seeks back and redelivers the failed record within the same consumer, so ordering on that partition is kept, but the partition is blocked during backoff; keep total backoff well under max.poll.interval.ms. Mapping to the same partition number requires the .DLT topic to have at least as many partitions as the source. The recoverer adds headers such as kafka_dlt-exception-message and kafka_dlt-original-offset, which makes replay tooling easy. For long, non-blocking retries use @RetryableTopic, which routes to retry topics instead, at the cost of losing strict per-key ordering. Pitfall: if the listener catches and logs exceptions itself, the handler never sees them and nothing reaches the DLT. Verify with an EmbeddedKafka test that throws on a specific key and asserts the record lands on orders.DLT. Cost is O(1) per record plus the retry attempts.

Combine a client-level timeout, a Resilience4j Retry and a CircuitBreaker with a fallback, so slow calls fail fast, transient errors are retried briefly, and a persistently failing dependency is cut off.

@Service
class PricingClient {
    private final RestClient rest;
    PricingClient(RestClient.Builder b) { this.rest = b.baseUrl("https://pricing.internal").build(); }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "pricing", fallbackMethod = "cachedPrice")
    @Retry(name = "pricing")
    public Price quote(String sku) {
        return rest.get().uri("/prices/{sku}", sku).retrieve().body(Price.class);
    }
    Price cachedPrice(String sku, Throwable t) { return priceCache.lastKnown(sku); }
}

resilience4j.circuitbreaker.instances.pricing.sliding-window-size=20
resilience4j.circuitbreaker.instances.pricing.failure-rate-threshold=50
resilience4j.circuitbreaker.instances.pricing.wait-duration-in-open-state=30s
resilience4j.retry.instances.pricing.max-attempts=3
resilience4j.retry.instances.pricing.wait-duration=200ms

Set the connect and read timeouts on the underlying HTTP client, for example 500 ms and 2 s through a ClientHttpRequestFactory, because TimeLimiter annotations only work with async return types. With the default aspect order Retry wraps CircuitBreaker, so each attempt is recorded by the breaker and an open breaker throws CallNotPermittedException without hitting the network. Retry only idempotent calls such as this GET, and configure retry-exceptions so 4xx responses are not retried. Expose the breaker state through the Actuator health and Micrometer metrics so you can alert when it opens. Overhead is O(1) per call, with a ring buffer of size 20 in memory.

Configure CORS inside the Spring Security filter chain, because a WebMvcConfigurer mapping alone runs after security, so the preflight OPTIONS request is rejected with 401 or 403 first.

@Bean
SecurityFilterChain api(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.cors(Customizer.withDefaults())
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated())
        .oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()));
    return http.build();
}

@Bean
CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration c = new CorsConfiguration();
    c.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("http://localhost:5173", "https://portal.example.com"));
    c.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS"));
    c.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "Content-Type"));
    c.setAllowCredentials(true);
    c.setMaxAge(3600L);
    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource s = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    s.registerCorsConfiguration("/api/**", c);
    return s;
}

Disabling CSRF is reasonable only for a stateless bearer-token API. Never combine allowCredentials true with a wildcard origin; Spring rejects that, and allowedOriginPatterns is the narrow escape hatch. For local development, a Vite server.proxy entry mapping /api to http://localhost:8080 is often simpler: the browser sees a same-origin call, so no CORS is involved, which mirrors production where nginx or an ingress serves the SPA and API under one domain. Verify with curl -i -X OPTIONS plus Origin and Access-Control-Request-Method headers and check the Access-Control-Allow-Origin response header.

Keep the access token in memory, let the refresh token live in an HttpOnly cookie set by the backend, and share one in-flight refresh promise so ten concurrent 401s trigger a single refresh.

let accessToken: string | null = null;
let refreshing: Promise<string> | null = null;

async function refresh(): Promise<string> {
  const r = await fetch("/api/auth/refresh", { method: "POST", credentials: "include" });
  if (!r.ok) throw new Error("session expired");
  accessToken = (await r.json()).accessToken as string;
  return accessToken;
}

export async function api(url: string, init: RequestInit = {}, retried = false): Promise<Response> {
  const headers = new Headers(init.headers);
  if (accessToken) headers.set("Authorization", `Bearer ${accessToken}`);
  const res = await fetch(url, { ...init, headers });
  if (res.status === 401 && !retried) {
    refreshing ??= refresh().finally(() => { refreshing = null; });
    try { await refreshing; } catch { window.location.assign("/login"); throw new Error("unauthenticated"); }
    return api(url, init, true);
  }
  if (res.status === 403) throw new Error("forbidden");
  return res;
}

A 401 means the identity is missing or expired, so refresh and retry exactly once; a 403 means authenticated but not allowed, so show an access-denied message and never refresh, otherwise you loop. The retried flag prevents infinite retries. Pitfall: storing tokens in localStorage exposes them to any XSS. On the Spring side, return 401 from the resource server's AuthenticationEntryPoint and 403 from the AccessDeniedHandler so the client can tell them apart.

Stop sending and rendering everything at once: paginate on the API and virtualise the list so the DOM only holds the visible rows.

import { useVirtualizer } from "@tanstack/react-virtual";
import { useRef } from "react";

export function OrderTable({ rows }: { rows: Order[] }) {
  const parentRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const v = useVirtualizer({
    count: rows.length,
    getScrollElement: () => parentRef.current,
    estimateSize: () => 36,
    overscan: 10,
  });
  return (
    <div ref={parentRef} style={{ height: 600, overflow: "auto" }}>
      <div style={{ height: v.getTotalSize(), position: "relative" }}>
        {v.getVirtualItems().map((item) => (
          <div key={rows[item.index].id}
               style={{ position: "absolute", top: 0, width: "100%", height: item.size,
                        transform: `translateY(${item.start}px)` }}>
            {rows[item.index].reference} {rows[item.index].status}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

Rendering cost drops from O(n) DOM nodes to O(visible) nodes, roughly 30 regardless of n. On the API, accept a Pageable and return Page<OrderDto>, or for deep scrolling use keyset pagination (where id > :lastId order by id limit 100), which stays fast where large OFFSET values degrade. Use stable keys, not array indexes, and memoise row components. Verify with the React Profiler and the Performance panel. Pitfall: virtualising but still filtering and sorting 10,000 items on every keystroke; debounce input or push filtering to the server.
